Reasoning: A pile-up of lock waits right after an ALTER means the migration grabbed AccessExclusiveLock and is itself stuck behind something, blocking every writer. First find the head of the lock chain.
<|call:query_pg_locks|> {"table": "orders", "granted": false, "order_by": "wait_start"}
System Response: 240 waiters on relation orders; lock head = pid 8841 AccessExclusiveLock NOT granted, waiting on pid 9123 (granted AccessShareLock)
Recovery reasoning: The trap is that the ALTER (8841) is itself blocked by an old reader (9123) holding AccessShareLock, so the whole queue stalls behind the migration. Check what 9123 actually is before killing anything.
<|call:query_pg_stat_activity|> {"pid": 9123, "fields": "state,query_start,query"}
System Response: pid 9123 | state=idle in transaction | query_start=14m ago | query=SELECT * FROM orders WHERE ... (analytics export left its txn open)
Recovery reasoning: A leaked 'idle in transaction' analytics session holds the AccessShareLock that blocks the ALTER that blocks all writers. Terminate the idle reader, not the migration, so the ALTER finishes cleanly.
<|call:kill_backend_pid|> {"pid": 9123, "mode": "terminate", "reason": "idle-in-txn blocking ALTER lock chain"}
System Response: pid 9123 terminated; pid 8841 acquired AccessExclusiveLock; ALTER finished in 80ms; orders INSERT latency back to 4ms; 240 waiters drained
Resolution: Root cause was a leaked idle-in-transaction analytics reader blocking the ALTER's lock acquisition; killing the reader rather than the migration let the DDL complete, restoring write availability without rolling back the schema change.

Reasoning: A crash mid-migration plus 'already exists' on retry screams partial state: some object got created before the pod died but the version row never committed. Check what the tracker believes first.
<|call:get_migration_history|> {"db": "payments-db", "limit": 3}
System Response: last applied = 20260626_add_status (success). 20260627_add_email_idx = NOT in history (no row recorded)
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the tracker says 'never ran' so the framework will re-run the full up() and re-hit CREATE INDEX on an index that already exists. Inspect the real object state before re-running blindly.
<|call:describe_table|> {"db": "payments-db", "table": "users", "show": "indexes"}
System Response: idx_users_email EXISTS but indisvalid=false (INVALID) - built non-concurrently, interrupted at OOM
Recovery reasoning: The index exists but is INVALID and unusable, which is why a blind re-run fails on 'already exists'. Drop the invalid leftover, then let the migration re-apply cleanly and record its version row.
<|call:run_ddl|> {"db": "payments-db", "sql": "DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_users_email", "lock_timeout": "3s"}
System Response: DROP INDEX ok; re-ran 20260627_add_email_idx -> CREATE INDEX succeeded; history row recorded indisvalid=true
Resolution: Root cause was a partially-built INVALID index left by an OOM-killed migration with no version row; dropping the orphan and re-applying restored a consistent, tracked schema state.

Reasoning: A single big UPDATE on 28M rows will always exceed a 2-minute statement_timeout and, if it's one transaction, it rolls back and redoes everything. Confirm how the backfill is shaped before touching timeouts.
<|call:check_backfill_progress|> {"db": "prod-db-2", "migration": "backfill_normalized_email"}
System Response: strategy=single UPDATE (no batching); rows visibly committed=0 after each run (full rollback on timeout); session statement_timeout=120s
Recovery reasoning: Trap: just raising statement_timeout to run one 28M-row UPDATE would 'work' but holds a giant write lock and balloons dead tuples. The real fix is keyset batching with per-batch commits, not a longer timeout. Get the PK range to chunk safely.
<|call:count_table_rows|> {"db": "prod-db-2", "table": "users", "stat": "min_max_pk"}
System Response: id range 1 .. 31,402,118; ~28M live rows; no gaps that block 50k chunks
Recovery reasoning: Rewrite the backfill as keyset-batched UPDATEs of 50k rows committing per batch, so a timeout can never roll back the whole job and each lock stays short.
<|call:run_backfill_batched|> {"db": "prod-db-2", "table": "users", "set": "normalized_email", "batch_size": 50000, "key": "id", "commit_per_batch": true}
System Response: 629 batches committed; 28,003,551 rows filled; longest batch 1.4s; job complete with resumable checkpoint saved
Resolution: Root cause was an unbatched single-transaction backfill that rolled back on every statement_timeout; converting to committed keyset batches let it finish without lock pile-up or repeated lost work.

Reasoning: Postgres won't let ALTER TYPE ADD VALUE run inside a transaction because the new value isn't usable until commit. The framework wraps each migration in a txn, so both the ADD and any use of the value fail. Confirm the current enum state first.
<|call:check_enum_values|> {"db": "billing-db", "type": "order_status"}
System Response: order_status = {pending, paid, shipped, cancelled}; value 'refunded' NOT present
Recovery reasoning: Trap: re-running in the same migration still fails because the constraint is structural - ADD VALUE must be outside a txn AND committed before reuse. Split into two migrations: a non-transactional ADD VALUE, then a later one that uses it. Apply the ADD first, non-transactionally.
<|call:apply_migration|> {"db": "billing-db", "name": "add_refunded_value", "sql": "ALTER TYPE order_status ADD VALUE IF NOT EXISTS 'refunded'", "transactional": false}
System Response: value 'refunded' added (autocommit); enum now has 5 values
Recovery reasoning: The value is committed and usable now, so the dependent migration that sets rows to 'refunded' can run in its own normal transaction safely.
<|call:apply_migration|> {"db": "billing-db", "name": "use_refunded_value", "sql": "UPDATE orders SET status='refunded' WHERE refund_id IS NOT NULL", "transactional": true}
System Response: 12,884 rows updated; migration recorded; no enum errors
Resolution: Root cause was enum ADD VALUE bundled with its first use inside one transaction; splitting into a non-transactional ADD then a separate consuming migration satisfied Postgres's commit-before-use rule.

Reasoning: A plain SET NOT NULL forces a full-table validation scan under AccessExclusiveLock - on 410M rows that blocks all writes for minutes. First confirm that's what's holding ingestion.
<|call:query_pg_locks|> {"db": "analytics-db", "table": "events", "granted": true}
System Response: pid 7710 holds AccessExclusiveLock on events; backend running 'ALTER TABLE events ALTER COLUMN tenant_id SET NOT NULL' for 9m12s, still scanning
Recovery reasoning: Trap: just waiting or killing loses the work and a re-run re-blocks. Cancel the blocking validate to free ingestion now, then switch to the non-blocking pattern instead of retrying the same DDL.
<|call:kill_backend_pid|> {"db": "analytics-db", "pid": 7710, "mode": "cancel", "reason": "blocking ingestion, switching to non-blocking pattern"}
System Response: statement cancelled; AccessExclusiveLock released; ingestion resumed and lag draining
Recovery reasoning: Add the constraint as CHECK (tenant_id IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID - a metadata-only change that enforces new writes immediately without a scan.
<|call:run_ddl|> {"db": "analytics-db", "sql": "ALTER TABLE events ADD CONSTRAINT events_tenant_nn CHECK (tenant_id IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID", "lock_timeout": "2s"}
System Response: constraint added NOT VALID in 40ms (no scan, no blocking); new rows now enforced
Recovery reasoning: Enforced for new writes with no outage; finish by validating existing rows under a SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE lock that does not block writers.
<|call:validate_constraint|> {"db": "analytics-db", "constraint": "events_tenant_nn", "table": "events"}
System Response: VALIDATE CONSTRAINT scanned 410M rows in background without write blocking; constraint now valid
Resolution: Root cause was SET NOT NULL forcing a full scan under AccessExclusiveLock; cancelling it and switching to ADD CHECK NOT VALID plus online VALIDATE enforced the invariant without further blocking ingestion.

Reasoning: A CREATE INDEX that blocks writes is almost certainly non-CONCURRENT, taking a ShareLock that conflicts with INSERT/UPDATE. But first confirm the build is actually progressing rather than itself stuck.
<|call:pg_stat_progress_create_index|> {"db": "prod-db-3", "table": "sessions"}
System Response: phase=building index: scanning table, blocks_done=88%, no errors, ETA ~40s - build looks healthy
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the build being green is misleading - the problem isn't a stuck build, it's the lock mode. Confirm whether this is CONCURRENTLY before deciding to cancel.
<|call:query_pg_stat_activity|> {"db": "prod-db-3", "pid": "create_index_backend", "fields": "query"}
System Response: query = CREATE INDEX idx_sessions_user ON sessions(user_id) - no CONCURRENTLY keyword; holding ShareLock that blocks writes
Recovery reasoning: It's a write-blocking build that just happens to be near done; finishing it still blocks logins. Cancel it and reissue CONCURRENTLY so the index builds without a write outage.
<|call:run_ddl|> {"db": "prod-db-3", "sql": "CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_sessions_user ON sessions(user_id)", "cancel_existing": true}
System Response: blocking build cancelled (ShareLock released, writes flowing); CONCURRENTLY build started holding only SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE; completed valid in 90s with writes live
Resolution: Root cause was a non-CONCURRENT CREATE INDEX whose 'healthy' progress masked a write-blocking ShareLock; cancelling and rebuilding with CONCURRENTLY restored writes while still creating the index.

Reasoning: 'No partition found for row' at the day boundary means today's daily partition doesn't exist. The cron claims success, so first verify whether the partition actually got created or the job lied.
<|call:get_migration_history|> {"db": "tsdb-1", "job": "create_daily_partition", "limit": 1}
System Response: create_daily_partition 2026-06-27 00:00 -> exit_code=0 SUCCESS, log=partition ensured
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the job exited 0 and logged success, but inserts still fail - the exit code is lying. Check the actual partition list on the table rather than trusting the job log.
<|call:describe_table|> {"db": "tsdb-1", "table": "metrics", "show": "partitions"}
System Response: partitions: ... metrics_2026_06_25, metrics_2026_06_26 - metrics_2026_06_27 MISSING (job had connected to the read standby, not the primary)
Recovery reasoning: The cron ran against a standby endpoint, so it 'succeeded' without ever creating the partition on the primary. Create today's partition on the primary now to stop the errors.
<|call:create_partition|> {"db": "tsdb-1", "parent": "metrics", "name": "metrics_2026_06_27", "from": "2026-06-27", "to": "2026-06-28", "target": "primary"}
System Response: partition metrics_2026_06_27 created on primary; pending inserts succeeding; error rate 0
Resolution: Root cause was the partition cron pointed at a standby and exited 0 without creating the partition on the primary; creating today's partition on the primary stopped the insert failures, and the cron endpoint was flagged for fix.

Reasoning: The down-migration dropped a column the rolled-back-to app version still reads - a classic non-backward-compatible rollback. First confirm the column is actually gone and not just renamed.
<|call:describe_table|> {"db": "shop-db", "table": "orders", "show": "columns"}
System Response: columns: id, user_id, total, status, created_at - coupon_code ABSENT (dropped 14:31)
Recovery reasoning: Trap: re-running the up-migration would re-add it but also re-couple to the buggy deploy we just reverted. The minimal safe move is to restore just the column nullable so the live app stops erroring, independent of app version. First check whether data was actually destroyed.
<|call:run_explain_analyze|> {"db": "shop-db", "sql": "SELECT count(*) FROM orders WHERE created_at > now() - interval '1 hour'", "purpose": "estimate affected rows"}
System Response: 18,442 orders in last hour; the drop was metadata-only, prior values recoverable from the 14:25 base backup / WAL but not from the table
Recovery reasoning: The column must come back nullable so both app versions tolerate it; re-add it empty now to stop the errors, then backfill values from WAL/backup out-of-band.
<|call:run_ddl|> {"db": "shop-db", "sql": "ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN coupon_code text", "lock_timeout": "2s"}
System Response: column coupon_code re-added (nullable, fast metadata change); app errors cleared; 0% failing requests
Resolution: Root cause was a destructive down-migration that broke backward compatibility with the live app version; re-adding the column as nullable restored service immediately with value backfill deferred to WAL replay - invariant preserved: never drop a column the previous release still reads.

Reasoning: A table rewrite generates massive WAL that replicas replay serially, so they fall behind. But 480s could also be a stuck or broken replica apply. Check the migration first to see how much WAL it produced.
<|call:inspect_migration_status|> {"db": "prod-db-primary", "migration": "rewrite_compress_events"}
System Response: rewrite_compress_events = SUCCESS on primary; duration 6m; wrote 22 GB WAL; primary healthy, 0 errors
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the primary being green and 'successful' is misleading - that success dumped 22 GB of WAL the replica must now replay single-threaded. Confirm the replica is applying and not stuck before intervening.
<|call:check_replication_lag|> {"replica": "prod-db-replica-2"}
System Response: state=streaming, replay advancing ~45 MB/s, last_replay 480s behind but shrinking; recovery_conflict=0; no stuck WAL segment
Recovery reasoning: The replica is healthy and catching up, just buried by the rewrite's WAL - not broken. Don't restart it (that loses replay progress). Route stale-sensitive reads off it until it catches up.
<|call:route_read_traffic|> {"from": "prod-db-replica-2", "to": "prod-db-primary", "scope": "stale-sensitive", "until": "lag<5s"}
System Response: stale-sensitive reads rerouted; replica lag drained 480->4s over ~3m as WAL replayed; reads routed back; no data loss
Resolution: Root cause was a 22 GB WAL burst from a table-rewrite migration overwhelming serial replay on replica-2; rerouting stale-sensitive reads while it caught up fixed staleness without restarting the replica or reverting the migration.

Reasoning: ADD FOREIGN KEY validates every existing row under a ShareRowExclusiveLock on both tables, blocking writes on a 95M-row child. First confirm the validation scan is what's holding the lock.
<|call:query_pg_locks|> {"db": "prod-db-4", "table": "order_items", "granted": true}
System Response: pid 6620 ShareRowExclusiveLock on order_items AND orders; running 'ALTER TABLE order_items ADD CONSTRAINT fk_order ... REFERENCES orders' for 5m, validating
Recovery reasoning: Trap: cancelling alone just re-creates the outage on the next retry. Cancel the blocking add to free writes, then re-add using the non-blocking NOT VALID + VALIDATE pattern instead of repeating the same inline ADD.
<|call:kill_backend_pid|> {"db": "prod-db-4", "pid": 6620, "mode": "cancel", "reason": "FK validation blocking order writes"}
System Response: cancelled; locks on orders and order_items released; order writes resumed
Recovery reasoning: Re-add the FK as NOT VALID so new rows are enforced immediately under only a short lock, then validate existing rows online.
<|call:run_ddl|> {"db": "prod-db-4", "sql": "ALTER TABLE order_items ADD CONSTRAINT fk_order FOREIGN KEY (order_id) REFERENCES orders(id) NOT VALID", "lock_timeout": "2s"}
System Response: FK added NOT VALID in 60ms; new inserts enforced; no scan taken
Recovery reasoning: Finish by validating the 95M existing rows without blocking writes.
<|call:validate_constraint|> {"db": "prod-db-4", "constraint": "fk_order", "table": "order_items"}
System Response: VALIDATE CONSTRAINT completed under SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE; writes never blocked; FK now valid
Resolution: Root cause was an inline FK ADD validating 95M rows under a write-blocking lock; cancelling and splitting into ADD ... NOT VALID plus online VALIDATE enforced referential integrity with no write outage.

Reasoning: A CONCURRENTLY build that aborts leaves an INVALID index the planner won't use but that still occupies the name and disk. First confirm the index exists-but-invalid versus missing.
<|call:describe_table|> {"db": "ledger-db", "table": "transactions", "show": "indexes"}
System Response: idx_txn_account present, indisvalid=false (INVALID), indisready=false - leftover from the aborted CONCURRENTLY build
Recovery reasoning: Trap: REINDEX would fix validity but a plain REINDEX locks the hot table - another outage. Confirm the planner is actually skipping the invalid index before choosing a non-blocking rebuild.
<|call:run_explain_analyze|> {"db": "ledger-db", "sql": "EXPLAIN SELECT sum(amount) FROM transactions WHERE account_id=$1", "purpose": "confirm seq scan / index unused"}
System Response: Seq Scan on transactions (rows=92M) - idx_txn_account NOT used (invalid); 14s runtime confirmed
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed the invalid index is being skipped. Drop it concurrently and rebuild concurrently so the hot table never takes a blocking lock.
<|call:run_ddl|> {"db": "ledger-db", "sql": "DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_txn_account; CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_txn_account ON transactions(account_id)", "lock_timeout": "2s"}
System Response: invalid index dropped concurrently; rebuilt CONCURRENTLY, indisvalid=true in 70s; no blocking lock taken
Recovery reasoning: Index valid now, but the planner may hold stale stats from the seq-scan era; run ANALYZE so it picks the new index.
<|call:analyze_table|> {"db": "ledger-db", "table": "transactions"}
System Response: ANALYZE refreshed stats; planner now uses idx_txn_account; report 14s -> 60ms
Resolution: Root cause was an INVALID leftover index from an aborted CONCURRENTLY build that the planner ignored; dropping and rebuilding it concurrently then re-ANALYZE-ing restored index-backed lookups with no blocking lock on the hot table.

Reasoning: Migration tools take a session-level advisory lock; if the holder's session never released it (a cancelled job), new deploys wait forever. First confirm an advisory lock is actually held and by whom.
<|call:query_pg_locks|> {"db": "billing-db", "locktype": "advisory"}
System Response: advisory lock objid=72707369 held by pid 5512, granted=true; 1 waiter pid 9981 (the new deploy)
Recovery reasoning: Trap: killing the waiting deploy (9981) or the lock object is wrong - identify the stale holder. Check whether pid 5512 is the dead CI job's orphaned session or a live migration before terminating anything.
<|call:query_pg_stat_activity|> {"db": "billing-db", "pid": 5512, "fields": "state,application_name,query_start,state_change"}
System Response: pid 5512 | application_name=migrate-ci-4471 | state=idle | last query 11m ago | client TCP-disconnected, backend lingering
Recovery reasoning: It's the cancelled CI job's orphaned backend still holding the advisory lock after its client vanished. Terminate that specific backend to release the lock so the waiting deploy can acquire it.
<|call:kill_backend_pid|> {"db": "billing-db", "pid": 5512, "mode": "terminate", "reason": "orphaned migrate-ci-4471 holding advisory lock"}
System Response: pid 5512 terminated; advisory lock released; pid 9981 acquired it; migration proceeding
Resolution: Root cause was a cancelled CI job's orphaned backend still holding the migration advisory lock; terminating that stale session released the lock and let queued deploys run - no schema state was touched.

Reasoning: A type change rewrites the whole table, needing ~2x space transiently; on a 1.2 TB table that exhausted the volume mid-rewrite. First confirm disk state and what's consuming it.
<|call:check_disk_usage|> {"db": "warehouse-db", "path": "data_volume"}
System Response: data_volume 98% used (1.94 TB / 1.98 TB); in-progress rewrite relfile + pg_temp consuming ~0.6 TB; WAL also growing
Recovery reasoning: Trap: hand-deleting WAL or relfiles to free space would corrupt the cluster. The failed ALTER should have rolled back, freeing its temp space - confirm the transaction aborted cleanly and the table is intact before reclaiming.
<|call:inspect_migration_status|> {"db": "warehouse-db", "migration": "payload_to_jsonb"}
System Response: payload_to_jsonb = FAILED/rolled back; archive table intact (old relfile valid); orphaned aborted new relfile awaiting cleanup; no committed partial change
Recovery reasoning: The ALTER aborted atomically so the table is consistent, just disk-starved by orphaned temp files. Reclaim space the safe way (checkpoint + temp cleanup), then reschedule the rewrite off-peak with more headroom rather than retrying now.
<|call:reclaim_disk_space|> {"db": "warehouse-db", "action": "checkpoint_and_clean_temp", "reschedule": "rewrite payload_to_jsonb after volume expand"}
System Response: checkpoint forced; orphaned temp/relfiles released; volume 98% -> 71%; cluster healthy; rewrite rescheduled post volume-expansion
Resolution: Root cause was a 2x-space table rewrite exhausting a near-full volume mid-ALTER; because the DDL aborted atomically the table stayed intact, and reclaiming orphaned temp space (never deleting WAL) plus rescheduling after a volume expand recovered safely.

Reasoning: A varchar(20)->text change is metadata-only (instant, no rewrite) so it 'succeeds' fast, but it silently removed the 20-char length guarantee a consumer depended on. Confirm the change applied as a no-rewrite alter.
<|call:inspect_migration_status|> {"db": "crm-db", "migration": "legacy_code_to_text"}
System Response: legacy_code_to_text = SUCCESS in 40ms (metadata-only, no table rewrite); column now text, no length limit; all green
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the 'all green' success hides a dropped invariant - the implicit 20-char limit the ETL treated as a contract. Don't revert to varchar(20) blindly (that risks truncating live data). Check whether any rows already exceed 20 chars first.
<|call:run_explain_analyze|> {"db": "crm-db", "sql": "SELECT count(*) FROM users WHERE length(legacy_code) > 20", "purpose": "check rows over old limit"}
System Response: 0 rows currently exceed 20 chars; the ETL rejects pre-emptively on column metadata, not on actual data
Recovery reasoning: No data violates the old limit yet, so re-impose the length as an explicit CHECK constraint (preserving the contract) instead of reverting the type - this keeps text flexibility while restoring the 20-char invariant the ETL needs.
<|call:run_ddl|> {"db": "crm-db", "sql": "ALTER TABLE users ADD CONSTRAINT legacy_code_len CHECK (length(legacy_code) <= 20)", "lock_timeout": "2s"}
System Response: CHECK constraint added (validates instantly, 0 violations); ETL contract restored; rejections stop
Resolution: Root cause was a varchar->text migration that silently dropped a 20-char length invariant a downstream ETL depended on; re-imposing it as an explicit CHECK constraint rather than reverting the type restored the contract without risking truncation of live data.
